WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:06.690
- All right. Hi, everybody.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.160
Thank you for coming. I'm Steve Pitti.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.050
I'm the Director of the RITM Center,

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.650
and faculty in history,
American studies, and ER&amp;M.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.830
We're really excited today
to host this showcase

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.190
of student research
from the summer of 2022.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.240
As everybody here knows,

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.180
these projects are some
of the many projects

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.720
that RITM had the chance
to support this past year.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.660
We sponsored 37 student research projects

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.520
in the summer of 2022.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.740
Those who've received funding,

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.650
including people in this
room, traveled far and wide,

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.760
deepened their work on
undergraduate and graduate projects,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.100
prepared for senior theses and MA theses,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.010
and worked on chapters of
their doctoral dissertations.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.630
We were delighted this year

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.550
to support students working
toward their degrees

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.340
in the Graduate School of Arts
and Sciences in Yale College,

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.740
and also in many of Yale's
professional schools.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.020
We've had the opportunity
over the last six years

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.070
to support the completion
of many student projects

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.890
by helping awardees to get to
archives and research sites

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.260
where they needed to be to
advance their scholarship.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.870
Past recipients of these research awards

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.730
have written prize-winning
dissertations and articles

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.640
and completed their degrees,
which is just as important.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.650
I'll mention too, to all of you,

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.690
that we're currently accepting research

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.470
and conference travel
applications in RITM for the fall

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.450
with an early October deadline.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.830
So, check out our weekly newsletter

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.110
for more information about
that if you haven't seen it.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.460
You can sign up for the newsletter

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.150
if you're not already
signed up at ritm.yale.edu

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.130
by clicking on the link on the webpage

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.960
that says, "Stay connected."

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.860
So today, we'll be hearing
about summer research projects,

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:41.693
as you know.

00:01:41.693 --> 00:01:45.150
Each of our presenters
has just three minutes

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.920
to describe their summer research.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:47.753
And then we'll,

00:01:47.753 --> 00:01:49.710
we'll then ask members of the audience

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.530
if any one person has a
brief question to ask,

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.660
and we'll give that
presenter a brief moment

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:55.950
to respond to that question.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.380
This is the interest of
keeping things moving,

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.970
and we will be moving fast

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.900
and demanding concision and clarity

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.480
from both presenters and audience members.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.020
But I really do hope that
everyone here enjoys themselves

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.260
and that you'll join us after
the program right out here

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.280
in the lounge area for some
relaxing food and drink,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.470
where we will not rush you
and hold you to three minutes

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.600
to finish your empanada or whatever

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.970
and that you'll continue
the conversation there

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.130
and ask more questions of one another.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.990
So, I think this should be a lot of fun.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.390
And with that, I wanna
turn this over to Amanda.

00:02:32.158 --> 00:02:35.241
(audience clapping)

00:02:41.962 --> 00:02:42.795
- Okay. Can I start?

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:43.628
Okay.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.620
Embarking on my summer internship

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.500
in Puerto Rico with the Archivo General,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.660
I wanted to find connections
between the island

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.750
and my specific region
of interest, Connecticut,

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.480
as it pertains to Puerto Rican diaspora.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.030
My reasons for going were
also a little selfish.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.190
Being a Puerto Rican
raised on the mainland,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.650
I've always wondered whether
I could claim Puerto Rico

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.540
as a site of belonging.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.100
Participating in this internship

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.350
then fulfilled both a personal dream

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.330
of spending extended time on the island

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.430
and a professional dream
of conducting research

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.110
in Puerto Rico.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.960
The internship with Archivo
General in San Juan,

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.210
a collaboration between Yale,

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.580
my alma mater, Rutgers University,

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:24.900
and the University of Puerto Rico

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.150
included five other student interns,

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.760
Ana Calderon, Gabriel Rivera Cotto,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.523
Rosa Cordero, Monica de
Jesus and Veronica Munoz.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.810
Led by director Hilda Ayala
and archivist Pedro Roig,

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.310
we sorted through documents

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:41.370
from the Puerto Rico Industrial
Development Corporation

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.340
or PRICO, which under Operation Bootstrap,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.830
provided tax exemptions
to mainland businesses

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.590
in the 1940s through the 1990s

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.990
that opened factories in Puerto Rico

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.000
to rapidly industrialize the island

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.980
and stimulate its economy.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.510
Multiple companies took advantage

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.490
of this colonial capitalist project,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.620
including quite a few from Connecticut.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:02.700
I found correspondence

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.310
with the Hamilton Manufacturing Company,

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.420
likely a recording equipment
manufacturer here in New Haven,

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.850
the M Hart Corporation in Hartford,

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.150
Union Carbide, which is a
chemical corporation in Danbury,

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.720
the US Time Corporation, or
watchmakers in Waterbury,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.020
and the Orange Pulp Company,
Valve Corporation of America,

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:24.660
and Warnaco Inc, all in Bridgeport.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:25.740
In addition to making

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.720
these Connecticut Puerto Rico connections,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.810
I also got to visit the (speaking Spanish)

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.290
at UPR's Rio Piedras campus in my off time

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.600
to explore one of my
other research interests,

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.330
Puerto Rican student activism
and education reform.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.510
I collected data of a
student-led strike in 1981

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.670
over tuition hikes at UPR,

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.130
data which I anticipate using
in a future journal article

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.483
or dissertation chapter.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.170
In all this, internship was
crucial to my research interest

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:53.400
in diasporic studies,

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.520
in that I had the opportunity
to challenge the narrative

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.770
of Connecticut as merely
the passive receiver

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.330
of Puerto Rican migrants.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.310
Rather, Connecticut actively shaped

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.890
the very socioeconomic
conditions in Puerto Rico

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.380
which prompted migration to the mainland.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.570
Likewise, gathering data
about the UPR strike

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.300
in 1981 enabled me to see the dynamism

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.450
of Puerto Rican student activism
in the late 20th century.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:16.770
And even though mainland

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.360
and island student activists were fighting

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.190
for change an ocean apart,

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.590
or really, a sea apart from one another,

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:25.800
overlaps in their respective
advocacy demonstrate potential

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.010
for intra-communal
influence, however indirect.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.803
Thank you very much for your time.

00:05:30.803 --> 00:05:33.886
(audience clapping)

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:36.833
- [Steve] Questions?

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:44.070
Amanda, why don't you talk a little bit

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.290
about one of the most challenging features

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.441
of the research work you did?

00:05:48.441 --> 00:05:50.430
- One of the most challenging features?

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:54.300
So, I think for me, Spanish
is my second language,

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.360
and while I knew going there

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.800
that I would need to brush up on it,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.250
I think for sure it was
a challenge, you know,

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.510
both really trying to get a grasp

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:04.800
of the documents I was looking at,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.740
and then also being able
to communicate confidently

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.180
with the fellow archivists.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.270
I think, the first couple
weeks were just kind of trying

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.430
to psychologically wrap
my head around, okay,

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.493
it's gonna take time for the
Spanish to kind of pick up.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.250
It's gonna take time for me
to really feel comfortable

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.650
with these archives and
that is part of this process

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.570
and it's an uncomfortable process,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:25.890
but I need to be okay with that process.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:27.210
So, yeah.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.536
- [Steve] Thank you.

00:06:28.536 --> 00:06:31.619
(audience clapping)

00:06:32.711 --> 00:06:33.570
- Hey, everyone.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:37.200
So, my tentative project is
called Corrugated Surrogacies,

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.360
Cardboard Kinships in the War in Vietnam.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.150
And it looks at the history
of transnational migration

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.920
and adoption through the everyday object

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:45.840
of the cardboard box.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.030
Projected here is a series of images

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:50.340
that sequence the
packaging and transporting

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:53.040
of Vietnamese orphans into
makeshift cardboard cribs

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.720
during Operation Babylift

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.303
at the end of the war in Vietnam in 1975.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.530
So, Operation Babylift is
often narrated in paternalistic

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:05.010
and legalized discourses of
familial rescue and sacrifice.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.830
Oftentimes, these narratives
take place as interviews with

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.380
or as oral histories
from adoptee survivors

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:11.790
of Operation Babylift

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.710
who have been able to
reunite with their families

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.290
and/or reflect upon their
experiences assimilating

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:17.700
to America.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:19.620
These stories bookend Operation Babylift,

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.653
starting with 1975 and
following now into the present.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.110
However, as much as these interviews

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:28.170
and oral histories index and
humanize refugee experiences

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:30.420
of memory, trauma and amnesia,

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:33.510
such genres of storytelling
are simultaneously delimiting

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.090
when considering the fact
that dozens of orphans

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.230
either died in transit

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.450
or that thousands of other
personal stories are lost

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:41.100
from the record as well.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.100
So thus, I took this summer
to turn to the cardboard box

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.590
as my primary archival object of choice,

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:48.900
as a material that continues
to linger and litter

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.050
in the landfills and military
bases across transpacific

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:55.440
in order to re-narrate the
history of Operation Babylift.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:56.640
By shifting our attention away

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.190
from the more obvious
contents of the cardboard box,

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.440
to instead the form of the crib itself,

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.300
I'm more interested in
extending this history

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.730
of Operation Babylift beyond 1975

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:07.620
as a means to trace a broader genealogy

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.380
of US militarism and its mechanisms.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:11.910
By shifting our focus from the babies

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:13.350
to the boxes themselves,

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:16.320
we are able to spot a
minor but telling detail,

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:17.730
minor but telling detail,

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.470
that of the V3C marker,

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.230
which is highlighted in the red circles.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:25.230
This V3C label index is a
military grade cardboard box

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.030
specifically commissioned by and designed

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.050
for the US government

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.690
in the efficient transportation
of wartime goods,

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.790
meaning that the cribs that
you see here that were used

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:35.760
to house the orphans were
and still are the same cases

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:37.920
that are used to haul wartime supplies

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:40.870
such as ammunition and lunchbox
rations since World War II.

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:44.790
This subtle detail led me to a
series of university archives

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.140
from Cambridge to New York

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.500
with the support of RITM
and the WGSS program,

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.270
through which I learned that,
throughout the 20th century,

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:52.890
the United States commissioned a series

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:54.480
of material science engineers

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.760
and business academics and nutritionists

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:59.370
to constantly optimize the
production of cardboard.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.080
Starting with the individual meal kits

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:02.160
the government constructed

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.890
to feed its soldiers since World War II

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:05.100
to the cardboard cribs used

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.890
to house its racialized victims
during the war in Vietnam,

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.900
the state sanctioned care
was not simply a matter

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.640
of ethics and emergencies.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:14.730
Instead, it also involved a
careful calculation of finances

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:16.680
as markets and militarism intertwine

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:19.380
in the management of imperial intimacies.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.360
As I continued to examine
how cardboard folds

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:22.770
across the history of empire,

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:24.150
I'm excited to continue this work

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.250
at the interdisciplinary intersections

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:29.100
between critical refugee
studies, supply chain studies,

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:30.420
and discard studies

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:32.310
as a means to understand
the ongoing effect

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.410
of cardboard as an ecological

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.960
as well as an epistemological structure.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.073
Thank you.

00:09:38.073 --> 00:09:41.156
(audience clapping)

00:09:50.407 --> 00:09:52.805
- [Audience Member] How did
you get into this research?

00:09:52.805 --> 00:09:56.305
How did you (indistinct)?

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.650
- Yeah, it was really serendipitous.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:03.090
It was a class that I took with Jess,

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:04.860
actually, he's right next to you.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:05.850
I studied the Vietnam War

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.790
normally through contemporary literature,

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:10.470
but it was just like looking at this,

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.390
we had an archival
project that we had to do,

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:14.730
like a quick like one
page story that we wrote,

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:15.990
and I just picked this image

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:17.340
'cause it was like the
first thing I could find

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:18.173
in my Google Drive.

00:10:18.173 --> 00:10:19.680
But then upon relooking at it,

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.140
like I noticed that these
boxes were all the same shape

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.330
and I wanted to move away

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.250
from more standard oral history methods

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:27.630
and speculative methods.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:30.295
So, yeah. Thanks so much.

00:10:30.295 --> 00:10:34.080
(audience clapping)

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:35.400
- Hi, my name is Ming.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.520
I'm a graduate student in
the Department of Psychology

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:40.530
and with support from the RITM,

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:41.820
I ran a study looking

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.610
at whether learning new
diagnostic information

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:46.170
about a person can change

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:49.800
people's initial face-based
implicit impressions

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.630
of competence for that person

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.120
and for people who share
similar facial features

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.520
and whether people do this
differently for targets

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.620
from different racial backgrounds,

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.270
and by implicit impressions,

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:03.480
we're referring to impressions
that are measured indirectly

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:06.660
and are less intentional
than explicit impressions,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:07.680
which are measured directly

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.020
by asking people about their opinions.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.100
So, in our study,

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:13.260
first, participants were introduced

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.660
to a competent looking target,

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:17.100
and depending on the condition,

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:20.190
the target was from one of
four different racial groups.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:21.090
They then learned

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:23.580
either low competence
behavioral information,

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.010
or neutral information about the target

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.200
and completed a modified version

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:30.780
of the affect misattribution procedure,

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:31.613
which was used

00:11:31.613 --> 00:11:34.530
to measure implicit
impressions of competence.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:35.610
Because of time constraints,

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:38.430
I'll only present what we
found for Black targets

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.410
and for white targets here.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:42.060
So, looking at the graphs,

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:46.020
the Y axis is the proportion
of more competent trials

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.540
in the affect misattribution procedure,

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:49.800
which you can just interpret

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:52.470
as people's implicit
impressions of competence.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:56.070
So, higher values
indicate that the person,

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:59.460
that people found the person
to be high on competence

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:02.640
and the X axis is the prime type

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:04.950
in the affect misattribution procedure.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:07.170
The target is denoted in green.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:10.170
Other competent looking faces are in red,

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.080
and control faces are in blue.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:15.690
So, the left half of both graphs is

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:19.290
when people learned low
competence behavioral information

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.160
about the target.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:21.480
And the right half is

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.970
when people learned neutral information.

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:27.330
So, if we first look at the
right half of both graphs,

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.520
we see that the pattern is very similar

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:31.020
for both Black targets

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:32.520
and for white targets.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:36.300
So when people learned neutral
information about the target,

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.070
they formed implicit impressions

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:40.620
of competence based on the face.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:41.760
So, both the target

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.400
and other competent
looking faces were seen

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.820
as implicitly more competent
than control faces.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:50.310
But when people learned
low competence information

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:51.150
about the target,

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:54.960
so now looking at the
left half of both graphs,

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.000
when the target was Black,

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.800
people did not change their impressions.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:01.740
They still had a high
implicit competence impression

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:04.950
of the target and of other
competent looking faces.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:06.750
However, when the target was white,

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:10.320
people did update their implicit
impressions of the target

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.170
in line with the new
behavioral information.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:15.660
But learning low competence
behavioral information

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.030
about one competent looking person

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:19.680
did not influence their impressions

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.260
of other competent looking faces.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:24.540
So, we're planning to run
another version of the study

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:27.720
with a larger sample to see
if the results would replicate

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:30.300
and to explore the possible mechanisms

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:31.740
behind this difference.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:32.874
Thank you.

00:13:32.874 --> 00:13:35.957
(audience clapping)

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:41.250
Yes?

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.130
- [Audience Member]
Thank you for this talk.

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:46.320
How are you measuring and
standardizing the information

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:47.760
that you were presenting?

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.909
How are you determining
what was low competence

00:13:49.909 --> 00:13:52.290
versus (indistinct)?

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.410
- So, we did a number of pretests

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:57.240
before running the main study.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:00.360
So, we came up with
four different vignettes

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:01.830
denoting low competence,

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:05.010
and we had independent raters on Prolific,

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:07.650
which is an online participant
recruitment platform

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:12.240
where we run our study to rate
the vignettes on competence

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.340
and then also a couple of other traits

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:15.783
which we controlled for.

00:14:18.512 --> 00:14:21.595
(audience clapping)

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:24.480
- Hi, y'all.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:26.280
So, in 1965, Al Raby,

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.290
a local civil rights leader in Chicago,

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:31.650
reflected on the last five
years of protest in a speech.

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:34.650
He said, "80 days and 72 marches ago,

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:37.380
the Chicago Freedom Movement
strode into high gear

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:40.230
when nearly 400 of us
occupied Chicago streets

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.900
and thoroughfare on our
way to tell Mayor Daley

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.210
to let superintendent Ben Willis go."

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:48.030
He continued, "I'm sure
that most of you realize

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.880
how important it is to
overcome here in Chicago.

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:51.750
If we win here,

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:55.200
it clears the way for liberation
of every Northern ghetto."

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:56.940
Raby, pictured here,

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.010
had spent the last five years as convener

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:03.000
of the Coordinating Council
of Community Organizations,

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.170
say that five times fast,

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:06.240
an umbrella group in
Chicago that coordinated

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:09.120
across the many civil rights
organizations in the city.

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:11.130
A former teacher in
Chicago Public Schools,

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:13.650
Raby was deeply devoted
to the power of education

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.420
as a liberatory mechanism.

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:18.750
He and others believed that
breaking into and approving,

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:21.510
improving Chicago's public
schools would lead to an opening

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:23.160
of the city more broadly.

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:25.490
But this fight for more equal
education didn't just start

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:27.180
in 1960.

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:29.460
In the Depression Era, so the 1930s,

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:32.280
Chicago schools took major
funding cuts across the board.

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:33.960
Schools in Chicago's Black neighborhoods,

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:36.900
mainly on the South Side of
the city, took the biggest hit.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:40.320
This led to groups like the
NAACP and the Urban League

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:43.110
to really start rallying
around the issue of schools.

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:44.610
So, three major factors were involved

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:46.200
in sort of their fight again or fight

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:47.850
for better schools, I guess.

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:51.780
The first is that most of
these schools were districted

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:53.250
in a way that they were segregated.

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:56.070
So, given the segregation of neighborhoods

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:56.903
at the time as well,

00:15:56.903 --> 00:15:58.020
it was sort of linked into that.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:01.050
So, they wanted to redistrict
the schools across the city.

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:02.550
The second issue was curriculum.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:04.260
So, same fight we're fighting now

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.630
for more true, honest
histories in classrooms,

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:07.463
things like that.

00:16:07.463 --> 00:16:10.770
And then finally, the schools
were incredibly overcrowded.

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:13.260
So, this was in part due to the influx

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:15.270
during the great migration of Black folks

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:17.100
and in part due to just
the lack of building,

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:18.750
especially sort of in that Depression Era

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:21.270
when there just wasn't the
funding to do that building.

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:23.520
And so, as a result of that overcrowding,

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:25.230
you saw a lot of schools on double shifts.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:26.820
So, that meant that half
of the students were going

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.310
to school in the morning,
half were going the afternoon,

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:30.210
and as you can imagine,

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:32.940
that left a lot of students
unsupervised throughout the day

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:34.200
and also missing, you know,

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.210
half of a day's education
across the board.

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:38.010
So, that was really one
of the biggest issues

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:39.330
and the issue that would hold through

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:41.520
through the rest of this movement.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:44.400
Throughout the 1950s, you see
tensions continuing to grow,

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.980
certainly with Brown v. Board in 1954.

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:49.170
You see it not only in
Chicago but nationally,

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:51.390
this sort of school
segregation issue becoming more

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:52.830
and more of a big deal.

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.740
And Chicago's school
superintendent was Ben Willis

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:56.910
at the time,

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:59.160
was sort of considered the main target

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:00.870
of the civil rights
movement during this period,

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:02.280
again, in Chicago in particular.

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:05.130
So, he came into the city in 1953.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:06.720
He built over a hundred schools.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:09.180
So, on the one hand, he was
touted as this great builder

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:13.320
and being called this Big
Ben the Builder by some.

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:14.520
But on the other hand,

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:17.040
his policies continued to
implement segregation in school.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:19.110
So he, instead of building a new school

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:19.980
in a Black neighborhood,

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.620
would put trailer classrooms
in the parking lot

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:24.600
or in the playground of that school

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:25.740
instead of building the new school.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:28.353
So, he certainly was like
continuing segregation.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:31.590
This all led to protests of Ben Willis,

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:34.920
daily marches on city
hall for an entire summer,

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:37.020
and in 1963 in October,

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:40.650
you see 250,000 students stay
out of school in a single day

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:41.820
for Freedom Day.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:43.590
Those students, some of
them just stayed home,

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.360
some of them went to Freedom Schools,

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:47.880
sort of touting back to Mississippi

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:48.930
and the Freedom Schools there

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:50.550
during the civil rights movement.

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:53.010
And so, that's sort of the big peak

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.110
of my own research is
this freedom day movement.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:58.650
So, I look at what came before
and what sort of came after.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:00.690
Basically, we see this
big mass movement coming

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:02.220
into this freedom day time.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.520
And after that, in 1966,
so a few years later,

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:07.590
Ben Willis, superintendent steps down.

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.600
Martin Luther King enters
the scene in Chicago,

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:11.400
starts fighting for fair housing,

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:13.680
and that's sort of how the story goes.

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:14.520
From there, we see,

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:16.680
obviously, schools are not
equal in Chicago still,

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.180
so the fight is still going,

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.520
but that's sort of the
civil rights era in a box

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.203
as far as Chicago schools go.

00:18:23.173 --> 00:18:26.256
(audience clapping)

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:32.250
Yeah?

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:34.140
- [Audience Member] Thanks
for this presentation.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:35.940
In studying the history

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:37.320
of like these sort of movements,

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:41.250
I was wondering if you
could see any sort of shared

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:44.190
or different positions
that student organizers

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:46.863
or parental organizers
played with each other

00:18:46.863 --> 00:18:49.500
when like organizing the
walkouts or organizing?

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:51.150
- Yeah, so there's like
a lot of different groups

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:51.990
that play in Chicago,

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:52.920
for sure and across the board,

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:54.750
but certainly in Chicago,
there's different neighborhoods,

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:55.583
there's different groups of people.

00:18:55.583 --> 00:18:57.180
You see teachers organizing.

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:58.230
You see students organizing.

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:00.000
You see parents organizing, you see,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.010
and then you sort of see
these like civil rights people

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:03.600
who they're like professional organizers,

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:04.620
some of them, right?

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:05.820
And so, with students,

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:08.760
I think you see them
fighting really particularly

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:10.230
in the later period for the most part,

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:13.170
so in the sort of '65, '66 era.

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:14.700
But you start to see
high schoolers starting

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:16.980
to fight really hard in those
issues of like curriculum

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.330
or they want better lunchrooms,

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:21.960
so really distinct things I
guess as opposed to these,

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:23.580
civil rights organizations
that are a little more broad

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:24.413
in what they want, right?

00:19:24.413 --> 00:19:26.370
They wanna change the policies
in the city and these other,

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:27.870
like the high schoolers, for example,

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:29.490
really wanna fix the one
thing that's happening

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:30.323
in their high school.

00:19:30.323 --> 00:19:32.813
So that's, I guess, the
biggest difference I would say.

00:19:34.955 --> 00:19:36.390
(audience clapping)

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:37.860
- So hi, everyone. I'm Anqi.

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:39.210
I'm a second year master student

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:42.630
in the council of East Asian
studies pursuing anthropology.

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:43.710
Today, I want to quickly walk

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:45.240
through my ethnographic field work

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:49.440
exploring African migrants'
experiences in Yiwu, China.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.720
So first, where and what is Yiwu?

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:53.670
Yiwu is one of the satellite cities

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:55.380
of the metropolis Shanghai.

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:56.520
It is not a big city,

00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:59.400
but it has the largest
wholesale market in the world

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.040
with over 75,000 stores.

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:05.520
Not surprisingly, Yiwu is a
gathering place for traders,

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.650
especially those from affluent countries

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:10.350
seeking cheap made-in-China goods.

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:11.700
In 2018, there were

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:15.513
over 3,000 African permanent
residents living there.

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:19.230
But the pandemic has changed many things.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:21.300
Many African traders were either deported

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:22.710
or voluntarily left

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:25.440
because of China's COVID
containment policies.

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.780
My own research was forced to end early

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:30.030
due to a sudden lockdown in the city.

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:31.920
Still, for one and a half months,

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:36.030
I worked with African
traders from Nigeria, Ghana,

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.100
Benin, Uganda, Somali and Senegal,

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:40.020
and used participant observation

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:41.640
and semi-structured interviews

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:44.610
to understand their migratory experiences.

00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:47.370
First, what my informant
and I discussed most

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:50.730
were the insecurities,
anxiety over mobility.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:53.880
China's travel ban has
prevented non-citizens

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:55.680
from entering the countries

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:58.710
while these traders
work hinges on mobility.

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:00.270
Running logistics businesses,

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:02.940
they used to receive African buyers flying

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:04.500
to China frequently.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:07.740
However, as no more buyers
are traveling to China

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:09.390
and online wholesale entities

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:13.410
such as Alibaba have expanded
into African countries,

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.830
many of these traders
expressed a sense of precarity,

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.800
seeing their position as
middle men destabilized.

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:22.140
But I found that, by
maintaining good reputation

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:24.690
and capitalizing on the mutual suspicion

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.600
between African clients and
Chinese business owners,

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.720
these middlemen were still
able to navigate the pandemic

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:33.480
because of their social capital.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:36.660
Race and racism also
emerged as central topics.

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:37.493
In my work,

00:21:37.493 --> 00:21:40.530
rather than treating
race as a static notion,

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:42.840
I study it as an interactive process

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:45.300
in which different groups
of people interacts

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:47.190
in a highly contingent manner

00:21:47.190 --> 00:21:50.460
that shapes the idea of
race and racial experiences.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:52.110
In Yiwu, I found that racism,

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:54.750
especially against African males,

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:57.090
has been linked to mythologies

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:59.730
about Black males' hypersexuality.

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:00.563
At the same time,

00:22:00.563 --> 00:22:03.510
even though many Chinese
business owners see the presence

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:07.980
of African traders as a hallmark
of China's globalization,

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:10.300
they also treat the presence of Blackness

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:13.263
as a sign of China's
temporary entanglements

00:22:13.263 --> 00:22:16.350
with the third world and even barbarianism

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:17.940
that needs to be eradicated

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:19.590
in order to fulfill their fantasy

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:21.930
of China becoming a first world country.

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:23.220
Despite my short time in Yiwu,

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:25.560
the topics I explore there are ones

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:28.590
that I will continue to
explore in my MA thesis.

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:30.306
Thank you so much.

00:22:30.306 --> 00:22:33.389
(audience clapping)

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:38.453
- [Audience Member] I just wanna know

00:22:38.453 --> 00:22:39.530
what the sort of challenges were

00:22:39.530 --> 00:22:40.650
of doing this research,

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:43.138
especially with COVID and everything else.

00:22:43.138 --> 00:22:46.690
- I think, in terms of travel ban policies

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:50.400
to overcame the 21 days,

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:53.130
21 days mandatory quarantine in hotel

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:55.020
and then go to the city

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:58.290
and then also facing the
sudden lockdown in the city.

00:22:58.290 --> 00:23:00.810
Yeah, so it was pretty
hectic, but at a same, I also,

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:03.270
I have to negotiate my positionality

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:04.830
as a female young scholars,

00:23:04.830 --> 00:23:09.780
because I constantly receive like warnings

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:14.780
from Chinese people regarding
the thing I talk about,

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:18.450
which is like African
males' hypersexuality.

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:21.060
So, actually, so that's why I think that,

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:23.970
rather than studying race
as a really static notion,

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:27.180
I wanna see like how
people interact together

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:31.860
and constructs the idea of
race and racism together.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:34.890
Yeah, though I think that is
two of my main challenges.

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:36.776
Yeah. Thank you.

00:23:36.776 --> 00:23:39.859
(audience clapping)

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:42.930
- Hi, everyone. My name's Babette.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:44.400
I am a second year

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:47.520
in the departments of African
American and American studies.

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:50.880
So, my work this summer actually
changed course quite a bit

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:52.590
from what I originally mapped out.

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:53.940
I was supposed to do some research

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:57.000
at this archival collection
at Fisk University

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:58.170
and had confirmed my appointment

00:23:58.170 --> 00:23:59.700
at the beginning of the summer,

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.260
but they had a change in management

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:03.090
and were no longer accepting appointments

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:04.620
until late fall.

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:08.160
So, I'm left with this
flight from JFK to Nashville

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:10.500
and this reservation at a three star motel

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:13.620
that I'm not really completely
sure what to do with.

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:14.490
I'm happy to say

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:16.410
that what I was able to
come up with is something

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:17.970
that I've been wanting to do for years

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:20.760
and that I think ultimately
really helped advance some

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:22.560
of my research on my analysis

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:24.240
of southern Black radio programs

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.730
in the mid to late 20th century.

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:27.960
But before I get into that,

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:29.340
I think it's important to back up

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:32.040
and give a little
background on my research.

00:24:32.040 --> 00:24:33.600
One of the main radio stations

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:36.210
that I look at in my research is WDIA,

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:39.390
which is the first all Black
programmed radio station

00:24:39.390 --> 00:24:44.130
to go on the air in the US in
1948 in Memphis, Tennessee.

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:47.280
So in my research, I
analyze recordings of WDIA

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:49.140
and draw up the intimate relationships

00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:52.500
between Black hosts and
Black Memphis listeners.

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.140
I analyzed these recordings
through the lens of affect

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.840
and haptic theory and
think about what it means

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.540
that WDIA provided material comfort

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.970
to those living amidst the
state sanctioned violence

00:25:02.970 --> 00:25:05.160
of a segregationist regime.

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:06.240
For example,

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:08.613
one recording in particular in
this collection of recordings

00:25:08.613 --> 00:25:11.010
that I listened to is an oral history

00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:13.620
with a woman named Jo Evelyn Grayson.

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.660
Jo Evelyn Grayson has now passed on,

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:18.210
but I believe she was 40 at
the time of this recording.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:21.810
And she talks about how, as
a kid growing up in Memphis,

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:23.820
WDIA is what she heard in the morning

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:26.460
as well as in the evening
when she went back to sleep.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:28.533
It completely framed her day.

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:32.730
Jo Evelyn Grayson was
born in 1948 in Memphis,

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:35.010
living in one of the five
or so Black neighborhoods

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:36.690
in Memphis at the time.

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:37.950
This is also the same year

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:40.170
that someone very special was born,

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:42.210
Black feminist scholar and literary

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:44.190
and cultural theorist, Hortense Spillers,

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:47.250
who was also born in a Black
neighborhood in Memphis.

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:49.290
During Professor
Spillers' time in college,

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:51.000
while she was protesting
for the integration

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:52.816
of diners through sit-ins,

00:25:52.816 --> 00:25:56.370
Hortense Spillers or Tensi
as her family calls her,

00:25:56.370 --> 00:26:00.150
also found the time to
be a radio DJ at WDIA.

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:01.890
She was one of the few
women to work at the station

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:03.270
at the time in the '60s

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:05.400
and she had a music segment every weekend

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:07.800
where she played songs
and talked on the air,

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:09.400
going by the name of Honey Girl.

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:12.300
Sitting in her Nashville home,

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:15.240
Professor Spillers has me
buckling over in laughter

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:17.760
at her impersonation of a
white manager at the station

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:21.480
with a big overbite who she
wasn't particularly fond of.

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:24.360
She provides me insight into
how she navigated the station

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:26.130
as a dark skinned Black woman

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:27.420
and how in some ways she felt

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:30.210
that the men at the station
actually protected her.

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:32.460
But she also confirmed
some of my suspicions

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:34.680
that the station was not
as outwardly political

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:35.820
as I had hoped,

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:38.340
that nobody other than
her really made statements

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:40.590
about segregation on the air.

00:26:40.590 --> 00:26:41.610
I'm so deeply grateful

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:43.560
to have spent this time
conducting this oral history

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:44.670
with Professor Spillers,

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:47.430
a fellow radio producer and DJ.

00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:49.440
I don't believe there's
any other published

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:51.630
or written work about her time at WDIA.

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:54.450
So, I'm excited to not only
include this in my research,

00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:56.550
but also to continue
working with the archivists

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:59.400
at Brown University to have
this oral history included

00:26:59.400 --> 00:27:01.710
with her collection of papers.

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:05.100
I was so happy to learn
also that Jo Evelyn Grayson,

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:07.170
the woman whose recordings
I have now listened to

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:08.550
for hours and hours

00:27:08.550 --> 00:27:11.100
and Professor Spillers had
actually known each other

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:14.070
and gone to school together
at Booker T. Washington High

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:15.930
and Memphis University.

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:18.870
She leaned back and said,
"Yes, of course I knew her"

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:20.160
when I brought up her name.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:21.763
Thank you so much.

00:27:21.763 --> 00:27:24.846
(audience clapping)

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:33.143
Yeah, go for it.

00:27:33.143 --> 00:27:35.100
- [Audience Member] This is
so exciting to hear about.

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:40.100
I was just curious, like does
WDIA have any like archives?

00:27:42.780 --> 00:27:45.810
- Yeah, so most of the collections

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:48.630
from WDIA are held at Indiana University,

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:49.860
and a lot of them are digitized,

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:50.693
which is really nice.

00:27:50.693 --> 00:27:53.253
So, the stuff of Jo Evelyn
Grayson is like online.

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:56.100
I don't think the radio station itself

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:57.750
has very well organized archives,

00:27:57.750 --> 00:27:59.913
but Indiana University has some of them.

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:02.280
Yeah. Awesome.

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:03.201
Thank you so much.

00:28:03.201 --> 00:28:05.490
(audience clapping)

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:09.300
- Hi, everybody. So
before, does this work?

00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:10.200
Oh, okay.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:11.190
Before I start,

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:14.400
I wanted to quickly just
give a brief background

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:19.173
to essentially my project and,

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:25.200
yeah, just kind of, and
also my, what I do here.

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:28.620
So, I am a second year PhD
student in anthropology.

00:28:28.620 --> 00:28:33.620
I also will be taking
the WGSS certificate.

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:40.130
So yeah, my larger
project, as you can see,

00:28:40.350 --> 00:28:42.060
so who, oh, you're actually starting?

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:42.960
Oh, okay.

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:44.280
I didn't realize. Okay.

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:48.210
So, my project is about Black
British gay and queer cis men

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:49.800
and how they negotiate space and place

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:50.640
in the gay community.

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:52.530
So, I'm interested in their experiences

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:54.960
within certain gay and queer spaces,

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:56.790
but also what it means to
be Black, British and gay

00:28:56.790 --> 00:28:57.960
in the 21st century,

00:28:57.960 --> 00:28:59.660
so their experiences more broadly.

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:05.780
So, I'm gonna be talking
particularly about this day

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:07.680
and this particular,

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:09.870
so this is just a picture
I got from Google.

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:12.270
So, but I was,

00:29:12.270 --> 00:29:13.500
yeah, so this is an old picture.

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:14.520
But anyway,

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:16.650
so, this particular thing I'm gonna talk

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:19.290
about is just research and fun

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:22.530
and a fun Saturday night
out in unexpected places.

00:29:22.530 --> 00:29:25.620
So, I didn't expect
this particular research

00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:26.610
that took place to happen.

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:29.610
And then for the people that I spoke to,

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:32.460
my interlocutors, this
was a Saturday night

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:34.860
in what I call an unexpected place.

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:38.040
So basically, it was a Saturday evening.

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:42.480
I, it was August, I was
about to come back to the US

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:44.550
and I needed a suitcase.

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:46.060
So, I went to Central London

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:48.870
and a particular place
called Oxford Street,

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:51.660
went to a particular
store, it was closing,

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:53.340
wanted to see if I could get big suitcase.

00:29:53.340 --> 00:29:54.173
There was no luck.

00:29:54.173 --> 00:29:55.920
So I thought, let me go down to Soho,

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:58.140
which is a particular area in London

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:01.590
and this is called Old Compton Street.

00:30:01.590 --> 00:30:02.423
So, there's a lot of gay,

00:30:02.423 --> 00:30:05.040
it's a gay area along here,

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:07.530
also, like just lots of gay
spaces and clubs and bars.

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:11.880
Anyway, so I was walking down the street,

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:14.100
I went to this bar and
I just stood outside

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:18.510
and was watching and
wanted to see if, you know,

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:19.680
I could interact with people.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:20.513
But it was very, very busy.

00:30:20.513 --> 00:30:22.680
It was late at night, like
10 o'clock in the evening.

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:24.840
Anyway, I decided to walk down this street

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:27.570
and just here, in the corner here,

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:29.340
I saw three Black people standing there,

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:31.530
so two Black guys and a girl.

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:32.820
And so, I walked past and I was like,

00:30:32.820 --> 00:30:33.690
I need to talk to them.

00:30:33.690 --> 00:30:35.520
But I was a bit, you know, scared.

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:37.860
So, I walked past and then I
came back and I spoke to them.

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:41.280
Anyway, we started
speaking and I, you know,

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:42.720
tell them about my research.

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:44.640
One of them was a bit, you know,

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:46.560
kind of like, not sure,
was looking at me like,

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:47.790
what's this guy talking about?

00:30:47.790 --> 00:30:49.470
And then another one was, you know,

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:51.390
a bit more enthusiastic,

00:30:51.390 --> 00:30:52.980
and the other one was
just a bit more quiet.

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:55.140
Anyway, I asked them what they were doing.

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:56.610
I told 'em about my project

00:30:56.610 --> 00:30:59.310
and they told me that they
were just standing there

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:01.980
like talking, having
drinks, blah, blah, blah.

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:04.200
There was an intention
to go out somewhere.

00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:07.890
Anyway, in the end, I found out
that they didn't go anywhere

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:09.450
and they stood there for the whole night,

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:11.490
just having a conversation, talking,

00:31:11.490 --> 00:31:12.840
having fun and drinking.

00:31:12.840 --> 00:31:15.660
So, basically the point of
this presentation today was

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:17.310
to essentially highlight the fact that,

00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:19.443
actually, you can find,

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:22.320
you can find research happen, sorry,

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:24.690
activity happening in spaces
that you don't expect.

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:26.010
So, I didn't expect to go

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:28.500
and find that people
will be here having fun.

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:30.180
They were, that was their night out.

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:32.850
They were in the corner
just kind of having fun.

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:35.490
Amongst a busy, busy night,

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:36.840
where so much was going on,

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:39.150
they were just there talking
to each other, having fun.

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:41.700
So essentially, I didn't
expect to find that.

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:44.550
And I think that's also
something that I've realized

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:46.230
in terms of like thinking
about space and place

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:48.360
and thinking about where
you're gonna find research

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:50.490
and where people enjoy themselves.

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:53.220
It's made me think anew
about those kind of concepts

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:54.690
and where you can find research

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:56.670
and where people might enjoy themselves.

00:31:56.670 --> 00:31:57.510
Because of the time,

00:31:57.510 --> 00:32:00.030
I can't go into too much
detail, but yeah, thank you.

00:32:00.030 --> 00:32:01.577
That's essentially what happened.

00:32:01.577 --> 00:32:04.660
(audience clapping)

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:11.770
- [Steve] Roy, did this
change any of your thinking

00:32:11.770 --> 00:32:12.783
about your project?

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:20.600
- Yes, it did, because I,
as I said, I didn't expect,

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:23.397
- Pick up the mic.
- Use the mic.

00:32:23.397 --> 00:32:24.393
- Oh, sorry.

00:32:24.393 --> 00:32:26.400
Yes, it did, 'cause I didn't expect,

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:29.163
as I was saying, to find,

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:31.710
I didn't think about the street

00:32:31.710 --> 00:32:34.740
as a site of research, basically.

00:32:34.740 --> 00:32:38.820
I, you know, I went initially to,

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:43.200
on this street, there's a like
a bar or club called G-A-Y,

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:45.180
which is quite famous actually.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:50.180
And I stood out, I was gonna go inside,

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:52.980
but I stood outside just
kind of like watching,

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:55.500
and yeah, just seeing
if I'd be able to speak

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:58.830
to people coming out or going in even.

00:32:58.830 --> 00:33:00.030
So anyway, there was no luck there

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:01.740
and I decided to just
walk around the street,

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:03.870
just to kind of observe what was going on.

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:07.290
And then I just saw those
three individuals there,

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:10.920
and that particular corner,

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:12.240
it was quite significant

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:14.640
because it was just in the
midst of so much going on,

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:15.960
they were just standing there.

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:19.890
You probably wouldn't
maybe even notice had,

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:20.850
if I wasn't paying attention,

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:22.290
I probably wouldn't have even noticed.

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:24.390
So, it has changed my mind in the sense

00:33:24.390 --> 00:33:27.210
of maybe going around, when
I walk on these streets,

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:28.043
to maybe I actually just speak to people

00:33:28.043 --> 00:33:29.850
on the street instead

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:31.200
and I never thought about that.

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:32.550
And so, it's got me thinking

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:34.870
about maybe observing more street action

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:37.800
because a lot of things actually
do happen on the street.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:40.380
So yeah, there was more
to the story as well,

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:42.270
but obviously, I didn't have as much time.

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:44.310
But it definitely has made me think anew

00:33:44.310 --> 00:33:47.610
about different spaces
in which you can, yeah,

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:49.980
research and maybe even
find interlocutors.

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:52.141
So, thank you.

00:33:52.141 --> 00:33:55.320
(audience clapping)

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:56.820
- Hi, everyone, my name's Thomas,

00:33:56.820 --> 00:33:59.520
and I'm a second year, and PhD,

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:02.853
in history of science and
medicine and early modern studies.

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:06.330
So, what if I told you
that poisonous snakes,

00:34:06.330 --> 00:34:08.130
infectious diseases like rabies

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:10.650
and transatlantic chattel
slavery all converged

00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:12.510
in the development and collection of some

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:15.990
of the world's first
standardized sets of biomarkers?

00:34:15.990 --> 00:34:18.300
These seemingly disparate
forces came into contact

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:20.190
with each other in the Colonial Atlantic,

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:22.260
and my project seeks to investigate

00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:25.110
and understand the fallout
of those encounters.

00:34:25.110 --> 00:34:26.700
For the purposes of today's talk,

00:34:26.700 --> 00:34:28.613
I ask us to specifically consider 16th

00:34:28.613 --> 00:34:31.680
to 19th century European
colonizing enterprises

00:34:31.680 --> 00:34:32.583
in the Caribbean.

00:34:33.510 --> 00:34:35.940
These historical events
brought many different people

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:37.800
from far flung places together,

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:41.190
and one of the key
ramifications of these meetings,

00:34:41.190 --> 00:34:43.020
from an epidemiological perspective,

00:34:43.020 --> 00:34:45.270
was the increasingly frequent outbreak

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:48.750
of seemingly novel diseases
from a European perspective.

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:50.100
In this light, the Caribbean,

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:52.860
a site of varied and intertwined
histories of domination

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:56.490
and subjugation of enslaved
peoples was often described

00:34:56.490 --> 00:34:59.440
as an inhospitable place filled
with disease and suffering.

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:03.390
But it was also a location
of great opportunity

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:05.790
from a European colonizing perspective.

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:07.500
And outside of familiar histories

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:08.940
of the search for gold and silver

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:11.940
or the establishment of a
brutal plantation complex,

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:14.550
the Caribbean also served as
an excellent testing ground

00:35:14.550 --> 00:35:17.340
for new medical theories and treatments.

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:19.380
The reasons for this were manifold.

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:21.270
There were a greater number of diseases

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:22.920
to examine than there were in Europe.

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:25.980
The enslaved African population
provided a ready-made class

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:28.620
of unconsenting test subjects,

00:35:28.620 --> 00:35:30.690
and the lush island topography presented

00:35:30.690 --> 00:35:32.190
plentiful new plants and animals

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:35.040
with which medical professionals
could fashion new drugs.

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:38.400
Therefore, Caribbean colonialism fostered

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:39.960
and encouraged medical innovation

00:35:39.960 --> 00:35:41.823
on a large scale, I argue.

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:44.820
The findings from my
recent archival research,

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:46.590
one page of which you
will see on the top right

00:35:46.590 --> 00:35:47.790
of the screen here,

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:50.640
demonstrate a concerted effort
to collect enormous sets

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:51.570
of medical data

00:35:51.570 --> 00:35:54.540
that linked patients'
biographical information

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:56.100
alongside their maladies,

00:35:56.100 --> 00:35:58.230
as well as data about the time of year

00:35:58.230 --> 00:35:59.520
and environmental conditions,

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:02.280
such as temperature and
barometric pressure.

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:05.250
These sources indicate a
sophisticated level of thinking

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:07.980
about the social and environmental
determinants of health

00:36:07.980 --> 00:36:09.660
in the Colonial Caribbean,

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:12.270
something that biomedicine
today still struggles

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:14.550
to get its head wrapped around.

00:36:14.550 --> 00:36:17.640
I also found more fraught
evidence of human experimentation

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:20.370
with poisons on terminally ill patients.

00:36:20.370 --> 00:36:22.860
Specifically, French
physicians conducted a series

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:25.260
of experiments with viper's
venom on rabies patients

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:27.690
in the 1760s, the findings of which

00:36:27.690 --> 00:36:29.550
eventually helped Louis Pasteur develop

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:31.650
the first rabies vaccine.

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:33.720
The venom was derived
from the lancehead viper

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:35.340
from Martinique, where it was used

00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:36.810
in exploitative experiments

00:36:36.810 --> 00:36:39.000
and to torture enslaved Africans.

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:42.120
Here we see how poison
was simultaneously a tool

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:44.070
of subjugation in the Caribbean

00:36:44.070 --> 00:36:46.740
and a miraculous medicament in Europe.

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:47.940
From this brief overview,

00:36:47.940 --> 00:36:50.340
I'll leave you with one simple question.

00:36:50.340 --> 00:36:52.020
How might these entangled histories

00:36:52.020 --> 00:36:54.150
of slavery, experimentation,

00:36:54.150 --> 00:36:56.880
and medical innovation
give us pause in believing

00:36:56.880 --> 00:36:59.340
in the infallibility
of the modern medicine

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:01.080
that they helped create?

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:02.021
Thank you.

00:37:02.021 --> 00:37:05.104
(audience clapping)

