00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:46.653
that I saw that really connected.

00:46:48.930 --> 00:46:50.907
Yeah, I'm trying to think of other things,

00:46:50.907 --> 00:46:52.320
but I think that's kind of one thread

00:46:52.320 --> 00:46:54.840
that I'm thinking a lot about right now.

00:46:54.840 --> 00:46:55.781
Thank you for asking.

00:46:55.781 --> 00:46:59.310
(audience clapping)

00:46:59.310 --> 00:47:00.840
- Hi, I'm Sylvia Ryerson.

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:04.350
I'm a fifth year PhD in American Studies

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:07.320
and the RITM Summer Research Fellowship

00:47:07.320 --> 00:47:09.390
supported three months of ethnographic

00:47:09.390 --> 00:47:11.580
and archival research
in Southwest Virginia

00:47:11.580 --> 00:47:14.490
and Eastern Kentucky for
my dissertation project,

00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:15.390
Calls from Home,

00:47:15.390 --> 00:47:18.390
Listening Past Carceral
Power in Central Appalachia.

00:47:18.390 --> 00:47:21.270
And so, from the end of May
until August, through August,

00:47:21.270 --> 00:47:22.890
I was based in Harlan County, Kentucky,

00:47:22.890 --> 00:47:25.830
pursuing multiple threads
of my dissertation project.

00:47:25.830 --> 00:47:28.830
And so, I wanted to share
this image for my presentation

00:47:28.830 --> 00:47:30.330
because one of the highlights

00:47:30.330 --> 00:47:31.830
of my summer was getting to work with

00:47:31.830 --> 00:47:34.500
and learn from the Higher
Ground Theater Company,

00:47:34.500 --> 00:47:37.470
which is based out of the local
community college in Harlan.

00:47:37.470 --> 00:47:39.960
And this is an image from their work,

00:47:39.960 --> 00:47:41.640
which I really think captures

00:47:41.640 --> 00:47:44.040
their community based
methodologies and practice.

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:47.220
And so, the man in the black
shirt is Bennie Massey,

00:47:47.220 --> 00:47:49.290
who is a former underground coal miner

00:47:49.290 --> 00:47:51.780
and a beloved community activist

00:47:51.780 --> 00:47:54.060
who lives in the small
town of Lynch, Kentucky,

00:47:54.060 --> 00:47:56.010
and who I was able to interview

00:47:56.010 --> 00:47:57.417
and learn from this summer.

00:47:57.417 --> 00:48:00.630
And so, Lynch was built by
the US Coal and Coke Company,

00:48:00.630 --> 00:48:03.150
which was a subsidiary of US Steel,

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:06.270
which was built as a company owned town

00:48:06.270 --> 00:48:10.440
to house coal miners and
their families in 1917.

00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:13.590
It was a segregated coal camp
that became the destination

00:48:13.590 --> 00:48:15.060
for thousands of Black families

00:48:15.060 --> 00:48:17.250
during the first wave
of the great migration

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:18.750
from the Deep South,

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:21.000
as coal company recruiters went to Alabama

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:22.470
and neighboring states to entice

00:48:22.470 --> 00:48:26.040
or coerce former sharecroppers
and convict lease miners

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:27.930
to come work as waged laborers

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:30.060
in the Kentucky coal fields.

00:48:30.060 --> 00:48:32.850
And despite unsafe,
exploitative and racist living

00:48:32.850 --> 00:48:34.050
and working conditions,

00:48:34.050 --> 00:48:36.510
Black miners and their
families turned this corner

00:48:36.510 --> 00:48:39.240
of Southeast Kentucky into
a powerful cultural center

00:48:39.240 --> 00:48:41.730
with deep roots in the region.

00:48:41.730 --> 00:48:43.560
And alongside my research in Lynch,

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:45.690
I actually was able to serve
as a teaching assistant

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:47.040
for a 12 week summer course

00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:49.350
that the Higher Ground
Theater Company was teaching

00:48:49.350 --> 00:48:51.033
in the Harlan County Jail,

00:48:51.990 --> 00:48:54.840
serving as an assistant for
their Appalachian studies class

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:57.420
and learning from and with the people

00:48:57.420 --> 00:48:59.790
in the Harlan County
Jail about the conditions

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:04.200
of their life and
incarceration in Harlan County.

00:49:04.200 --> 00:49:06.660
And so, the support from RITM
really made it possible for me

00:49:06.660 --> 00:49:09.780
to be in the community for
such an extended amount of time

00:49:09.780 --> 00:49:12.300
and really able to work
closely and collaboratively

00:49:12.300 --> 00:49:15.120
with the scholars and
artists in the region

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:16.500
who are doing such important work

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:20.160
and who made so many of my
own connections possible.

00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:21.810
And additionally, I was very fortunate

00:49:21.810 --> 00:49:24.990
to receive an RITM
conference travel award,

00:49:24.990 --> 00:49:26.730
which supported my travel to Indiana

00:49:26.730 --> 00:49:27.900
to give a paper presentation

00:49:27.900 --> 00:49:31.320
at the American Political
Histories Conference

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:33.540
titled the Past, Present and Future

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:37.320
of American Democracy
at Purdue University,

00:49:37.320 --> 00:49:41.310
where I presented as a part
of a paper panel session

00:49:41.310 --> 00:49:44.070
on Appalachia and energy politics

00:49:44.070 --> 00:49:45.570
with other Appalachian scholars.

00:49:45.570 --> 00:49:46.829
Thanks.

00:49:46.829 --> 00:49:49.912
(audience clapping)

00:49:54.300 --> 00:49:55.487
- [Steve] Sylvia, could
you say a little bit more

00:49:55.487 --> 00:49:57.533
about the project?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:49:57.533 --> 00:49:59.730
And so, I sort of had to jump between two,

00:49:59.730 --> 00:50:00.900
really, I was sort of,

00:50:00.900 --> 00:50:02.730
the research I was doing
in Lynch is sort of part

00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:04.110
of chapter two of the dissertation

00:50:04.110 --> 00:50:06.060
and then teaching in the
Harlan County Jail is part

00:50:06.060 --> 00:50:07.170
of chapter five.

00:50:07.170 --> 00:50:08.790
And it's looking at these different nodes

00:50:08.790 --> 00:50:10.620
of the carceral state
in Central Appalachia,

00:50:10.620 --> 00:50:12.720
including state prisons, federal prisons,

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:13.890
the county jail,

00:50:13.890 --> 00:50:15.420
and tracing that back to the roots

00:50:15.420 --> 00:50:18.715
of convict lease labor in Alabama.

00:50:18.715 --> 00:50:22.770
And so, and obviously
the afterlives of slavery

00:50:22.770 --> 00:50:25.440
and many other systems of
extraction in the region.

00:50:25.440 --> 00:50:27.810
And so, I think, in a way,

00:50:27.810 --> 00:50:29.580
what I was trying to balance

00:50:29.580 --> 00:50:31.530
and what sort of both
histories are present

00:50:31.530 --> 00:50:34.650
in Harlan County is both these deep roots

00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:39.650
of Black migration into Appalachia

00:50:41.280 --> 00:50:44.460
and sort of how race relations in the,

00:50:44.460 --> 00:50:47.730
at the turn of the century
structured labor relations

00:50:47.730 --> 00:50:49.200
in Appalachia throughout the century.

00:50:49.200 --> 00:50:51.480
And then also how, you know,

00:50:51.480 --> 00:50:55.380
those foundations have
also created the conditions

00:50:55.380 --> 00:50:57.750
of possibility for the
kind of jail expansion

00:50:57.750 --> 00:50:59.200
that's happening there today.

00:51:00.762 --> 00:51:03.845
(audience clapping)

00:51:04.800 --> 00:51:05.633
- Hello.

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:15.990
My name's Josh. I'm a
second year history PhD.

00:51:15.990 --> 00:51:20.730
My research focuses on
modern intellectual history

00:51:20.730 --> 00:51:24.840
and specifically the
changing debate about race

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:27.360
and the place of Jews in post-war Europe

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:30.300
among largely francophone intellectuals

00:51:30.300 --> 00:51:31.740
roughly in the timeframe

00:51:31.740 --> 00:51:34.650
between the Algerian War of Independence

00:51:34.650 --> 00:51:38.880
and the 1967 war in Israel Palestine.

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:43.880
I used my RITM summer travel
grant to go to a conference

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:46.830
that was called Hijacking
a Memory in Berlin.

00:51:46.830 --> 00:51:49.380
It was, according to the organizers,

00:51:49.380 --> 00:51:51.960
probably one of the first conferences held

00:51:51.960 --> 00:51:56.960
in post-war Germany that
directly discussed the Holocaust

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:01.890
and colonial violence
together on the same stage.

00:52:01.890 --> 00:52:06.270
This is a major taboo in German culture.

00:52:06.270 --> 00:52:11.270
It's because there is a
complicated political legacy

00:52:11.370 --> 00:52:13.680
about relativizing the Holocaust.

00:52:13.680 --> 00:52:14.730
In German culture,

00:52:14.730 --> 00:52:16.620
the Holocaust is the greatest evil

00:52:16.620 --> 00:52:17.940
that humanity ever carried out

00:52:17.940 --> 00:52:19.860
and nothing can ever be compared to it.

00:52:19.860 --> 00:52:23.400
And in some places,
intellectuals who compare,

00:52:23.400 --> 00:52:26.970
say, the ethnic cleansing of
Palestine during the Nakba

00:52:26.970 --> 00:52:31.970
or German violence in its African colonies

00:52:33.300 --> 00:52:38.300
to the Holocaust actually can
face really serious academic

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:40.320
and professional consequences.

00:52:40.320 --> 00:52:41.153
So, there was a little bit

00:52:41.153 --> 00:52:42.840
of an (speaking in
foreign language) element

00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:44.760
to the conference,

00:52:44.760 --> 00:52:49.757
but there was also like
proximate political reasons

00:52:50.940 --> 00:52:52.020
for it being held there as well,

00:52:52.020 --> 00:52:53.250
which is that, in Europe,

00:52:53.250 --> 00:52:55.830
the new right has sort
of flipped the valence

00:52:55.830 --> 00:52:56.910
of anti-Semitism.

00:52:56.910 --> 00:53:00.480
So, whereas say before 1980,

00:53:00.480 --> 00:53:01.800
what it meant to be on the far right

00:53:01.800 --> 00:53:05.160
in Europe was to be bluntly anti-Semitic.

00:53:05.160 --> 00:53:08.700
Now, the xenophobic far right in Germany

00:53:08.700 --> 00:53:11.730
and in France and
elsewhere, Poland, Hungary,

00:53:11.730 --> 00:53:14.820
has used a kind of phylo-Semitic argument

00:53:14.820 --> 00:53:17.610
to block the flow of immigrants from,

00:53:17.610 --> 00:53:20.100
largely from the Middle East.

00:53:20.100 --> 00:53:22.860
So for instance, in Germany, the AfD,

00:53:22.860 --> 00:53:26.340
which is in some sense
the sort of successor

00:53:26.340 --> 00:53:28.620
of neo-Nazi parties has opposed

00:53:28.620 --> 00:53:32.250
the German government's immigration plans

00:53:32.250 --> 00:53:34.020
to have Syrian refugees come,

00:53:34.020 --> 00:53:35.760
on the grounds that the
Syrian refugees are going

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:38.793
to bring in anti-Semitism into Germany.

00:53:40.020 --> 00:53:43.680
And so, all of this was being
discussed at the conference

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:48.060
and what ended up happening
was that the controversy

00:53:48.060 --> 00:53:49.590
that the conference was meant to address

00:53:49.590 --> 00:53:51.780
actually blew up during the conference.

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:55.290
And two Polish academics
criticized publicly

00:53:55.290 --> 00:53:56.760
and went to the German press

00:53:56.760 --> 00:53:59.280
over the remarks of a Palestinian scholar

00:53:59.280 --> 00:54:03.900
named Tareq Baconi who
explicitly compared the Nakba

00:54:03.900 --> 00:54:05.730
to the Holocaust.

00:54:05.730 --> 00:54:10.320
And this kind of, there
was a media fire storm

00:54:10.320 --> 00:54:12.390
in Germany over this.

00:54:12.390 --> 00:54:15.780
Right wing German politicians
called for the defunding

00:54:15.780 --> 00:54:17.340
of the venue where the
conference was held,

00:54:17.340 --> 00:54:19.740
which is called the
House of World Cultures.

00:54:19.740 --> 00:54:24.270
And I ended up covering
this for a magazine

00:54:24.270 --> 00:54:26.250
that I work for called Jewish Currents,

00:54:26.250 --> 00:54:27.720
which the article was called,

00:54:27.720 --> 00:54:29.550
The Challenge of Defending
Memory in Germany,

00:54:29.550 --> 00:54:32.820
which was about the sort of
new politics of anti-Semitism

00:54:32.820 --> 00:54:35.250
or anti anti-Semitism
and how they're connected

00:54:35.250 --> 00:54:39.060
to xenophobia and Europe immigration

00:54:39.060 --> 00:54:41.013
and the legacies of colonialism.

00:54:42.270 --> 00:54:43.103
And that's it.

00:54:43.103 --> 00:54:46.153
(audience clapping)

00:54:53.108 --> 00:54:53.941
Go ahead.

00:54:55.472 --> 00:54:58.170
- [Audience Member] This
is two prong question.

00:54:58.170 --> 00:55:00.930
What happened to the (indistinct)

00:55:06.117 --> 00:55:10.552
and do you, in terms of Germany's stance,

00:55:10.552 --> 00:55:13.410
do you know other (indistinct)

00:55:13.410 --> 00:55:15.515
compared to the Holocaust,

00:55:15.515 --> 00:55:19.620
I wonder if you could maybe
talk about why that might be,

00:55:19.620 --> 00:55:21.360
considering you mentioned things

00:55:21.360 --> 00:55:24.120
that happened in East Africa and genocides

00:55:24.120 --> 00:55:25.498
that happened before that,

00:55:25.498 --> 00:55:29.400
and why that might maybe how they navigate

00:55:32.130 --> 00:55:34.230
those kind of histories,

00:55:34.230 --> 00:55:35.780
the histories that came before?

00:55:37.020 --> 00:55:40.590
- Yeah, so Tareq was, ended up being fine,

00:55:40.590 --> 00:55:42.900
and a lot of the conference participants

00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:46.110
who were there signed a petition
kind of in support of him.

00:55:46.110 --> 00:55:47.370
And there ended up,

00:55:47.370 --> 00:55:49.080
it turned out that he had
a lot of support there

00:55:49.080 --> 00:55:49.913
and it did,

00:55:49.913 --> 00:55:53.640
I don't think it had severe
consequences on his life.

00:55:53.640 --> 00:55:56.190
He runs a Palestinian think
tank called Al-Shabaka

00:55:56.190 --> 00:55:57.330
which you should all check out

00:55:57.330 --> 00:55:58.470
if you're interested in that sort of thing

00:55:58.470 --> 00:55:59.820
where they are probably
at the cutting edge

00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:01.470
of Palestinian politics.

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:05.370
As for like the German sort
of relativization debate,

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:08.310
this sort of, it blew
up in the '80s at first

00:56:08.310 --> 00:56:10.680
because initially the
comparisons were between,

00:56:10.680 --> 00:56:14.790
was it possible to compare
the Soviet gulag system

00:56:14.790 --> 00:56:15.870
to the Nazi camp system?

00:56:15.870 --> 00:56:17.970
That was like what was sort
of the first historians debate

00:56:17.970 --> 00:56:20.610
in Germany, and that's
kind of where things were.

00:56:20.610 --> 00:56:23.910
And you know, generally it
was a far right talking point

00:56:23.910 --> 00:56:28.170
to compare because right wingers who maybe

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:30.600
or maybe not had some kind of
Nazi affiliation were saying,

00:56:30.600 --> 00:56:31.920
look, the communists also had camps.

00:56:31.920 --> 00:56:33.990
So it's not just the Nazi thing,

00:56:33.990 --> 00:56:34.980
it was also a,

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:37.500
it's like there were
bad guys on both sides.

00:56:37.500 --> 00:56:40.140
That's the background for why the politics

00:56:40.140 --> 00:56:42.180
of comparison is very hard.

00:56:42.180 --> 00:56:47.180
But also, the part of
Germany's rehabilitation

00:56:47.580 --> 00:56:49.350
into the Family of Nations

00:56:49.350 --> 00:56:52.440
after its unification has entailed

00:56:52.440 --> 00:56:55.290
the German state being explicitly Zionist.

00:56:55.290 --> 00:56:59.370
So, part of the Germans,
what Germany conceives of

00:56:59.370 --> 00:57:01.830
as its national interest is the protection

00:57:01.830 --> 00:57:05.820
of the sovereign State of Israel.

00:57:05.820 --> 00:57:07.353
And so, that makes,

00:57:08.250 --> 00:57:11.670
say, discourse about the
Palestinians or really any kind

00:57:11.670 --> 00:57:15.153
of anti-colonial politics,
not just kind of,

00:57:16.650 --> 00:57:19.590
it makes it a threat to
what the state has conceived

00:57:19.590 --> 00:57:23.103
of as its raison d'etre, basically.

00:57:24.535 --> 00:57:27.618
(audience clapping)

